\[
\boxed{
\hat{\sigma}^2
=
\frac{\mathrm{SSE}}{n-p}.
}
\]
The independence of \(\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}\) and \(\mathrm{SSE}\) allows us to replace the unknown \(\sigma\) with \(\hat{\sigma}\) and obtain exact \(t\) inference. The same framework extends naturally to:

linear combinations of regression coefficients;
general linear hypotheses;
confidence intervals for the mean response;
prediction intervals for future observations.

