ImportantBack-transformation changes the target



If \(\varepsilon\mid x\sim N(0,\sigma^2)\), then
\[
\operatorname{median}(Y\mid x)=\exp(\beta_0+\beta_1x),\qquad
\mathbb{E}(Y\mid x)=\exp(\beta_0+\beta_1x+\sigma^2/2).
\]
Exponentiating a fitted log response gives a fitted median under these assumptions, not the conditional mean on the original scale. The percentage change in the median is \(100\{\exp(\beta_1)-1\}\%\). The same mean ratio applies only when the error distribution is unchanged with \(x\). A Gaussian model for \(\log(Y)\) is also different from a Poisson GLM with a log link: the latter models \(\log\{\mathbb{E}(Y\mid x)\}\) directly and allows zero counts.













